#import "../lib.typ": *

#index-start("Stringere")
Stringere#gl("stringere") is perhaps one of the most ambiguous concepts in Italian rapier fencing.
One part of this might be due to the lack of good English (or German) translations of the word.
In English one often finds 'gain', or 'find', or 'stringer'.
Some teachers also use 'constrain' which, while not a direct translation, gets at the nature of the action.
I use either 'gain' or the italian 'stringere', although the latter results in somewhat awkward passive constructions.
This document does not try to make sense of how the concept is presented in different sources.#footnote[David Biggs#index[Biggs, David] has written an excellent #link("https://davidbiggs.substack.com/p/so-just-what-is-a-stringere")[blogpost] (#cite(<biggs2025JustWhatStringere>, form: "year")) trying to do just that.]
Instead, I have tried to describe how I think about, and teach, stringere.
The first section describes my notion of stringere---what it is, what it does, and how it does it.
The second section contains the drills I use to teach it.
If I teach the topic on its own, I usually spread it across two classes, but I use many of the same drills when working on other concepts, reviewing the principles of stringere in the process.

=== Principles of Stringere<stringere_principles-of-stringere>

#set quote(block: true)
David #index[Biggs, David] #cite(<biggs2025FindGainStringere>, form: "prose") summarises the technique of stringere as: #quote()[to gain an opponent’s blade, you want to place a greater length of your blade over a lesser length of theirs. And you want to turn your true edge - the edge you would usually try to cut someone with - toward their blade, and aim your point at their silhouette or just past it.]

While there are fine points of stylistic difference (some turn the true edge toward the ground, for example), this summarises most of the mechanical details.
It does not, however, explain why one would want to perform a stringere in the first place.#footnote[He does so elsewhere however @biggs2024GainingOpponentsBlade@biggs2024GainingOpponentsBladea@biggs2024GainingOpponentsBladeb@biggs2025FindGainStringere.]

On my view, stringere is an attempt at reducing a multivariate problem to one of geometry.
The larger problem is this: to gain control over the opponent's blade and limit their options, without giving them an impetus or a useable tempo, all while staying protected.
On its own, this puts pressure on the opponent, but a successful stringere also puts the fencer in a position to increase that pressure.

One detail I would want to add to Biggs' description is that one should position one's blade such that it crosses the opponent blade at, or slightly below, a 45\u{00B0} angle vertically and horizontally.#footnote[Some describe this as a 'double cross'---a cross in the horizontal plane and one in the vertical plane. I prefer to think of it as a cross in a diagonal plane.]
When done right, this not only makes direct attacks impossible, it also narrows the opponent's options considerably.

===== Common Mistakes
I see two common mistakes when someone is learning stringere.
One is for the fencer to accidentally give the opponent an opening, often their weapon arm, while trying to create the right angle.
The solution is to keep the hilt of the rapier between one's arm and the opponent's blade.
The other is for the fencer to bump or apply pressure to the opponent's blade.
Displacing the opponent's blade in this way#footnote[Later methods might call it 'divertimento'#gl("divertimento")] can be very useful for provoking a reaction, but that is something one can build on top of a good stringere.

A note on footwork: I teach stringere with a gathering step#index[Gathering step].
This is by no means the only way of doing it, but I find that it works well as a set-up for countering most common reactions to a stringere.

=== Drills<stringere_drills>

These are the drills I use when teaching the topic of stringere.
If I teach the topic on its own, I usually divide it into two classes.
The first class covers drills Ia.--IIb.; the second class reviews drills Ic. and IIa., and covers IIIa--e.

I do each drill for three rounds on each side (inside/outside): first round very slowly, second round at a pace that feels smooth, third round pushing the pace just beyond what is comfortable.
Contrary to other topics, where I will let pairs of students work on a drill for a longer period of time, I have each pair do a drill one time each before rotating to a new partner.

#[
  #set enum(numbering: "Ia.")
  + Basics.
    + A gives a line; B performs a stringere with a gathering step; if A sees an opening for a direct attack at any point, A strikes.
    + As Ia., but B's gathering step must bring them into middle range. If A sees no opening for a direct attack, B strikes with a thrust in opposition#gl("thrust-in-opposition").
    + As Ib., but if A sees an opening for a direct attack at any point, they strike.
  + Common reactions to a stringere.
    + A gives a line; B performs a stringere with a gathering step; A performs a disengage (cavazione#gl("cavazione")) and a thrust in opposition.
    + As Ib., when B attacks, A counters _stesso tempo_#gl("stesso-tempo"), with or without a void#gl("void"), with or without an empty-hand parry.
  + Counters to IIa.
    + As IIa.; B counters _a dui tempi_#gl("dui-tempi").
    + As IIa.; B counters _stesso tempo_ with a void.
    + As IIa.; B counters _stesso tempo_ with without voiding, with or without an empty-hand parry.
    + As IIa.; B attacks into A's disengage with a thrust in terza#gl("terza") (stoccatta#gl("stoccatta")).
    + As IIa.; B counters with a counter-disengage (contracavazione#gl("contracavazione")) and a thrust in opposition.
]
#index-end("Stringere")
